  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(C).$",
  "worked_solution": "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(C)=det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\cdot (-1)(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-4)\\cdot (-1)(12-5) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
